<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chur is the capital of the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near the routes to the Alpine passes. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, recorded from 451, ruled much of the region until the Ilanz Articles of the 1520s, and the town led the League of God's House within the Three Leagues. It is one of the oldest towns in Switzerland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chur.jpg" alt="Chur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amarco90 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Chur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chur lies in the Grisonian Rhine Valley, where the Rhine turns north, on the right bank of the Plessur at 594 m above sea level, almost entirely surrounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. It is reputedly the oldest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, with settlement going back to the Pfyn culture. It stands where the roads from several Alpine passes, among them the Lukmanier, Splügen and San Bernardino, come together and continue down the Rhine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered the area in 15 BCE as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. In the late 3rd century Curia Raetorum, later Chur, became the capital of Raetia prima. In the 4th century it became the seat of the first Christian bishopric north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and the first known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> is Asinio in 451. Otto I granted the right to collect tolls in 952, and in 1170 the bishop became a prince-bishop. The League of the House of God was formed in Chur in 1367 to resist the bishopric and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a>. After a fire in 1464, Emperor Frederick III freed the city from the bishop's power, and in 1465 the citizens gave all government to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>.</p><p>The League joined the Grey League and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a> in 1471 to form the Three Leagues. From 1523 Johannes Comander preached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in St Martin's Church, and by 1525 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> had fled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilanz-articles">Ilanz Articles</a> of 1524 and 1526 allowed each resident of the Three Leagues to choose a religion and sharply reduced the bishop's power. The Three Leagues became the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a> in 1803, with Chur as its capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chur is the capital and largest town of the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a>, or Grisons, and lies 120 km by rail from Zürich. German is the main language, with Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romansh">Romansh</a> also spoken. The cathedral of the Assumption was built between 1154 and 1270 on the site of a church of the first half of the 5th century. The church of St Luzi, first recorded in 821, became a Premonstratensian monastery in 1149.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1367 the cathedral chapter, the town of <b>Chur</b> and the communes of the Engadine, Bergell and other valleys under the Prince-Bishopric of Chur met to prevent Bishop Peter Gelyto from handing his temporal rule to the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-gods-house">League of God's House</a></li><li><blockquote>A bishop of <b>Chur</b> is recorded in 451, making it one of the oldest dioceses north of the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-chur">Prince-Bishopric of Chur</a></li><li><blockquote>A common diet met in rotation at <b>Chur</b>, Ilanz and Davos.</blockquote><a href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a></li></ul></section>
